@demographics
go-to: menu

= Demographic Groups

**Industrial Working Class**: Concentrated in São Paulo, Rio de Janeiro, and the industrializing south. Organized through the CGT and PTB-aligned unions. Politically conscious, increasingly radical. Roughly 20% of the population — small by European standards, but strategically decisive.

**Rural Laborers** *(bóias-frias, moradores, meeiros)*: Landless or near-landless agricultural workers, concentrated in the Northeast and the São Paulo interior. The most exploited class in Brazil. The peasant leagues of Francisco Julião have begun to organize them. Enormous revolutionary potential — roughly 35% of the population.

**Peasants and Smallholders**: Independent small farmers, sharecroppers, tenant farmers. Highly varied politically. In the Northeast they lean toward the leagues; in the south and center-west they tend toward conservatism or Catholicism. Roughly 20%.

**Urban Middle Class**: Civil servants, professionals, students, shopkeepers. Politically volatile — the base of UDN liberalism but also of left-wing student movements (the UNE). About 15% of the population. The student movement is disproportionately important.

**Landowners and Bourgeoisie**: Large landowners (*latifundiários*), industrialists, and financiers. Politically organized through the UDN and conservative PSD factions. Small in number, enormous in power. Terrified of agrarian reform. Roughly 5%.

**The Military**: Not a demographic in the electoral sense, but a political actor of the first importance. The officer corps is divided between *nacionalistas* sympathetic to Goulart and *golpistas* aligned with the UDN and ESG (Superior War College) doctrine. The enlisted ranks are a different matter — many are sons of the working class, and some will refuse orders when the moment comes.

**Catholics**: Brazil is overwhelmingly Catholic — over 90%. The Church is itself divided, between the conservative hierarchy aligned with landowners and the progressive wing beginning to develop what will become Liberation Theology. Dom Hélder Câmara represents the latter tendency. This division matters enormously for our work in the countryside.

